# EXPECT: splices a module into the program
#
# THE POINT OF THIS FIXTURE is the OTHER half of "the module dict is a snapshot
# of what RAN". Its sibling, module_conditional_binding.eigs, gates which
# bindings a module CREATES. Nothing asked whether the module FINISHES.
#
# The AOT splices a module's statements into the program, so a module top-level
# `return` became a bare `return 0` in main -- returning from the WHOLE PROGRAM,
# before the module dict was even constructed. Both halves were silent:
#
#   VM   ["a"] / 1 / null          AOT   NO OUTPUT AT ALL, rc 0, empty build log
#
# and the dict the code would have built invents `b`, which is the round-10 bug
# reached by a second road. `if` and `return` are each individually emittable,
# so nothing else in the compiler refused it.
#
# Measured cost of refusing: 0 of the 77 stdlib modules.
#
# A `return` at the MAIN program's top level is a different thing and is still
# supported -- there it really does mean "end the program", which is what the
# emitted `return 0` does. t101_toplevel_return.eigs gates that side.

import _r1_mod
print of (keys of _r1_mod)
print of _r1_mod.a
print of _r1_mod.b
